{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.068,
            0.817,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.845,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "105"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.885,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "What do you observe? The longer the chord, the closer it is to the centre. Let us try to understand why this is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.152,
            0.886,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 8:** Let AB and DE be two chords of a circle with centre C. Suppose $A B \\geq D E ,$ . Then the distance from C to AB is less than the distance from C to DE."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.216,
            0.886,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "As always, we draw the figure. Remember: by ‘distance from C to AB’ we mean the perpendicular distance. So, we drop perpendiculars CF and CG from C to AB and DE, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.294,
            0.414,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.244,
                0.491,
                0.323,
                0.51
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.16"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.453,
            0.294,
            0.632,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": "**Given:** AB > DE."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.32,
            0.706,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "**To show that:** CF < CG."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.344,
            0.877,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": "**Why is this true?** In Fig. 5.16, both AC and CD are radii. So, AC = CD. By the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras Theorem, $\\mathrm{CD^{2} = CG^{2} + GD^{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{AC^{2} = CF^{2} + AF^{2}}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.428,
            0.737,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\mathrm{CF^{2}+AF^{2}=CG^{2}+GD^{2}}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.454,
            0.876,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": "Now AB is greater than DE. So AF is greater than GD (because F and G are the midpoints of AB and DE)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.516,
            0.828,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": "Since $\\mathrm{AF^{2} \\geq GD^{2}}$ we have $\\mathsf { C F } ^ { 2 } \\leq \\mathsf { C G } ^ { 2 }$ So, CF < CG."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.582,
            0.886,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "**Comment:** The chord nearest to the centre is the chord containing the centre. Its distance to C is zero! So the diameter is the greatest chord."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.64,
            0.886,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "If one pushes the chord away from the centre, at some stage the chord becomes a single point, its length is zero, and the distance of C from this chord is the radius."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.733,
            0.622,
            0.762
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 5.5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.78,
            0.885,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": "1. Find the length of the chord of a circle where the radius is 7 cm and perpendicular distance is 6 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.829,
            0.886,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "2. Explain why the following statement is true: If the perpendicular distance of a chord from the centre is d and the radius is r, then the chord length is $2 \\sqrt { r ^ { 2 } - \\boldsymbol { d } ^ { 2 } }$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
